RISE ASCII SCENE 7
#############################################################################################################
#                                      RISE Ascii Scene File                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#   File:           misc_geometry_stress.RISEscene                                                          #
#   Description:    A REGRESSION INSTRUMENT for the two expressive-geometry chunks with the THINNEST test    #
#                   coverage in the corpus:                                                                 #
#                     - `cartesian_disk_geometry`, which had ZERO test scenes anywhere before this file      #
#                       (its only in-repo consumer was the guilloche watch hero, which is far too heavy to   #
#                       serve as a regression check)                                                        #
#                     - `path_instances_geometry` with a NON-TRIVIAL template on a CURVED path (the only     #
#                       other coverage, sweep_instances.RISEscene, instances plain spheres)                  #
#                                                                                                           #
#   LAYOUT: BACK band (z = -5), left to right --                                                            #
#                   CHECKER disk / FINE displaced disk / COARSE displaced disk                              #
#           FRONT band (z = +5), left to right --                                                           #
#                   UPRIGHT bead chain / SLANTED + SCALED bead chain                                        #
#                                                                                                           #
#   SPECIMENS (what it stresses -> what CORRECT looks like):                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#     CHECKER disk, back band left -- the LINEAR CARTESIAN UV, which is the whole reason this geometry       #
#       exists instead of a polar fan.  u = (x + R) / 2R, v likewise, so a checker in UV must land as a      #
#       true SQUARE GRID in world space.                                                                    #
#       CORRECT: a circular disk carrying a regular grid of EQUAL SQUARE cells, the same size at the centre  #
#       as at the rim, with the grid lines straight and axis-aligned and the circular edge cutting across    #
#       them.  A REGRESSION to polar UV shows immediately as wedge-shaped cells converging on the centre     #
#       (and cells that shrink toward the middle).  The disk's own normal is +Z, so it faces the camera.     #
#                                                                                                           #
#     FINE displaced disk, back band centre -- the DOCUMENTED reason the chunk exists: a flat Cartesian      #
#       base with an arbitrary 2D field displaced onto it via `displaced_geometry` with `uv_seam_fold        #
#       FALSE` (the field is an OPEN Cartesian map, so there is no closed-surface UV mirror to fold).  The   #
#       field is a concentric-RING term (5 rings) plus a 4 x 4 square-GRID term, chosen because the two      #
#       stress different things: the rings expose any radial UV distortion, the grid -- which reads as a     #
#       gentle four-fold SQUARING of the rings, strongest along the two diagonals -- exposes any             #
#       non-uniform cell density.  mesh_n 300.                                                                #
#       CORRECT: the rings are CONCENTRIC and EVENLY spaced from centre to rim -- they must NOT bunch up     #
#       near the middle -- the relief is equally crisp at the centre and at the rim, and the four-fold       #
#       squaring is symmetric.  A Cartesian disk's world-space cell size is constant everywhere; a polar     #
#       fan's is not, and would crowd the relief into the middle.                                           #
#                                                                                                           #
#     COARSE displaced disk, back band right -- the IDENTICAL field on the IDENTICAL base at mesh_n 44       #
#       instead of 300.  This makes the sampling lattice itself visible.                                     #
#       CORRECT: the same pattern, blocky, resolved on a visible SQUARE lattice -- and the disk's RIM is a   #
#       STAIRCASE of whole grid cells, not a smooth polygon.  That staircase is the Cartesian disk's         #
#       signature: the grid is built first and clipped to the circle, so the boundary follows the LATTICE.   #
#       A smooth polygonal rim at low mesh_n would mean a polar fan had been substituted.  The pair also     #
#       proves `mesh_n` is honoured at all rather than clamped to a fixed density.                           #
#                                                                                                           #
#     UPRIGHT bead chain, front band left -- `path_instances_geometry` with a NON-TRIVIAL TEMPLATE: a        #
#       `lathe_geometry` turned finial, not a sphere.  The template goes through the universal               #
#       TessellateToMesh contract, so this also checks that a lathe survives being consumed as a template     #
#       WITHOUT losing its turned profile.  The path is a genuinely 3D Catmull-Rom curve (it rises, falls    #
#       and swings through z), instances are laid at ARC-LENGTH `pitch 2.60`, and the template's +Y axis --  #
#       which for a lathe is its axis of revolution -- aligns with the path TANGENT, so the finials are      #
#       THREADED ON THE PATH like beads on a wire rather than standing up off it.  Instances BANK with the   #
#       path (there is no world-up mode).                                                                    #
#       CORRECT: a row of finials, EVENLY spaced along the ARC -- they must neither bunch in the tight bends #
#       nor stretch on the straights -- each lying along the curve with its wide bead leading and its        #
#       narrow neck trailing, and with the turned bead-cove-bead profile legible on every single one.        #
#       Spacing that varies with curvature means the walk is parameter-based rather than arc-length; a       #
#       featureless ellipsoid instead of the turned profile means the template was proxied rather than       #
#       tessellated.                                                                                         #
#                                                                                                           #
#     SLANTED + SCALED bead chain, front band right -- the SAME template on a COPY of the same path, at the  #
#       same `pitch 2.60`, but with `slant 55` and `scale 1.30` against the left chain's `scale 2.00`.       #
#       Slant rotates the template about the frame NORMAL, so the finials tip over sideways relative to the  #
#       tangent; scale sizes them uniformly.                                                                 #
#       CORRECT: the same number of finials at the same arc-length spacing as the left chain, every one of   #
#       them visibly TIPPED by the same angle, and all of them uniformly SMALLER.  A slant that varies       #
#       along the chain means the frame normal is drifting; a different COUNT means `scale` is leaking into  #
#       the arc-length walk.                                                                                 #
#                                                                                                           #
#   No specimen here should produce a log line.  A clean render leaves RISE_Log.txt free of any `Error` or   #
#   `Warning` entry.                                                                                        #
#                                                                                                           #
#   Author:         Aravind Krishnaswamy                                                                    #
#                                                                                                           #
#############################################################################################################

standard_shader
{
	name			global
	shaderop		DefaultDirectLighting
}

pixelpel_rasterizer
{
	max_recursion		10
	samples				16
	lum_samples			3
}

file_rasterizeroutput
{
	pattern				rendered/misc_geometry_stress
	type				PNG
	bpp					8
	color_space			sRGB
}

film
{
	width			1500
	height			620
}

pinhole_camera
{
	location			0 13 32
	lookat				0 1.5 0
	up					0 1 0
	fov					17.0
}

